 - `.rama/screens.json`: which screens House of Rama photographs, and who each is for.
 - `db/schema.ts`: the tables. `db/connect.ts`: the product's own database, when House of Rama connects one;
   without one (the tests, a build) a building reads its sample rows from `data/<table>.json`.
+
+## Tables and the database
+
+- `orders` (in `db/schema.ts`) holds the stall's orders: item, cups, price of a cup in paise, and when it was
+  taken. It keeps one day, so every order in it is today's. Like every table it carries the provenance columns.
+- `db/orders.ts` is how a building reads it. When `database()` is connected (the running app), it reads the
+  table; the first time the table is empty it writes in the sample day (four orders) with `createdBy: 'sample'`
+  and `siteId: siteId()`. When `database()` is null (the tests, `npm run build`), it reads the same four
+  orders from `data/orders.json`. No table is created and no migration is run here: House of Rama makes the
+  tables from `db/schema.ts` before the app starts.
+- A page that reads a table is an `async` component and exports `const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`, so it reads
+  when it opens, not when the app is built.
+- All amounts are kept in paise and shown in rupees with two places, like ₹45.00.
